sections.s2_technical:
Let me go through each strategy the way I would explain it to a junior choosing a key strategy for a new table.

`GenerationType.IDENTITY` leans on the database's auto-increment feature. The column is defined as `SERIAL`/`IDENTITY` on PostgreSQL or `AUTO_INCREMENT` on MySQL, and the database assigns the value during the INSERT. The id is not known until the row is inserted.

```java
@Id
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
private Long id;
```

The important consequence is that Hibernate CANNOT do JDBC batch inserts with IDENTITY, because it must execute each INSERT individually to read back the generated key. For high-volume inserts this is a real throughput limitation.

`GenerationType.SEQUENCE` uses a database sequence object. Hibernate asks the sequence for the next value, and crucially it can pre-allocate a block using `allocationSize`, so one sequence call yields many ids, which it then assigns to entities in memory. This keeps batch inserts working and reduces round trips.

```java
@Id
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = "acc_seq")
@SequenceGenerator(name = "acc_seq", sequenceName = "account_seq", allocationSize = 50)
private Long id;
// one "SELECT nextval('account_seq')" hands out 50 ids → fewer round trips, batching works
```

This is my default on PostgreSQL and Oracle because it batches well and the id is known before flush.

`GenerationType.TABLE` emulates a sequence using a dedicated table (for example `hibernate_sequences`) that stores the last-used value per entity. Hibernate reads and increments that row under a lock. It is portable to any database, but the row lock serialises id generation and makes it the slowest option. I only use it when the database genuinely has no sequence support.

```java
@Id
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.TABLE, generator = "acc_tab")
@TableGenerator(name = "acc_tab", table = "id_keys", pkColumnName = "key_name",
                valueColumnName = "next_val", allocationSize = 50)
private Long id;
```

`GenerationType.AUTO` delegates the choice to the provider based on the dialect. On modern Hibernate with PostgreSQL this often resolves to a sequence. It is convenient but I prefer to be explicit in production so the behaviour does not change silently when a dialect or Hibernate version changes.

`GenerationType.UUID` (standardised in JPA 3.1) or a Hibernate `@UuidGenerator` produces a 128-bit UUID in the application, with no database round trip needed to obtain the id.

```java
@Id
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.UUID)
private java.util.UUID id;
```

UUIDs shine in distributed systems and when you want the id before touching the database, for example to return it immediately or to use it as an idempotency key. The senior caution is that a RANDOM UUID version 4, used as a clustered or primary-key index, scatters inserts across the B-tree and causes page splits and index fragmentation, which hurts write throughput especially on InnoDB where the primary key IS the clustered index. The fix is a time-ordered id — UUIDv7 or ULID — which keeps inserts append-friendly while remaining globally unique.

Two final nuances. First, with IDENTITY, Hibernate must flush an INSERT immediately when you call `persist`, because it needs the generated id; with SEQUENCE and UUID it can defer. Second, `allocationSize` on a sequence must match the actual sequence increment, or you risk gaps or collisions; I keep them aligned and accept that ids may have gaps, which is fine for a surrogate key.

